Subject: [PATCH] wayland: focus the window through xdg-activation on request

Nothing outside gamescope can focus its host window except through compositor-specific window
management (wlr-foreign-toplevel, KDE's plasma-window-management); GNOME offers a foreign client none
at all. The portable mechanism is xdg-activation: whoever wants a window focused holds an activation
token (a desktop launch carries one in XDG_ACTIVATION_TOKEN) and the window's own client presents it.

Bind xdg_activation_v1 in the Wayland backend and add IBackendConnector::Activate(token). A client of
the nested X server requests it through a root property, GAMESCOPE_XDG_ACTIVATION_TOKEN, whose value is
the token: steamcompmgr reads and deletes it in one request and activates the current connector. An
empty value asks gamescope to request a token itself, the way a browser does when another process asks
it to show a page: stamped with the serial of the last input event (enter, button or key) on the
backend's own wl_seat, named alongside it. The backend now records button and key serials on that seat
for this; input is still handled on the input thread's seat. gamescope holds more than one wl_seat and
each gets its own serials for the same event, and a host may validate against the first-bound seat only
(Hyprland does), which is the backend's. Whether a token earns focus or only an urgency hint is the
host's policy (Hyprland: urgency only when the serial is not a recent one of ours).
